      "text": "that didnt seem to do anything. This is my doc:\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[table]{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{array}    % Required for better text alignment\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx} % Required for inserting images\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\setlength{\\parindent}{0pt}    % no paragraph indentation\n\\setlength{\\parskip}{1em}      % add vertical space between paragraphs\n\\usepackage[a4paper, margin\u003d1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{nicematrix}\n\\usepackage[table]{xcolor}\n\\newcommand{\\var}[1]{\\texttt{\\seqsplit{#1}}} \n\\usepackage{seqsplit} \n\n\\title{Discretized, First-Order-Kinetics Simulation of the Catabolism Steps of Major Macronutrients of a Peanut in the Human Body}\n\\author{Jon Sharp \\\\\n    jon.sharp@ucdconnect.ie \\\\\n    UCD Student Number: 25278680\n    }\n\\date{November 27, 2025}\n\n\\usepackage[style\u003dauthoryear,backend\u003dbiber]{biblatex}\n\n\\addbibresource{references.bib}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle \n\n\\section{Introduction}\nThis paper presents a software simulation prototype of the catabolism of key peanut macronutrients within the human body. The simulation begins with chosen quantities of starch, proteins, and fats in the mouth. It concludes in the mitochondria, with final outputs of produced ATP or energy equivalents from the Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle and Electron Transport Chain (ETC). While the simulation does not include major anabolic steps which influence the fate of peanut nutrients, this paper includes a brief discussion of these omitted pathways.\n\nTo construct this simulation, successful enzymatic reaction rate models have been selected from the literature. These included first-order, pseudo-first-order, and zero-order models. Individual catabolic steps are intended to appear realistic. However, the project prioritized learning and qualitative demonstration over quantitative accuracy. As such, as discussed below, strong assumptions made for the simulation model fail to capture true complexities of metabolism.\n\nFurther sections address simulation construction, models used in each organ and metabolic process, assumptions, and limitations.\n\n\\section{Simulation Construction}\n\\subsection{Micronutrient Inputs}\n\\label{sec:micronutrient-inputs}\nTo represent a peanut beginning ingestion in the mouth, quantities of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins corresponding to 50 grams of whole peanuts were selected. For simplicity, the model only considered the most prominent of each of these macronutrients in peanuts -- starch, oleic acid (mono-unsaturated) \\autocite{settaluri_peanuts_2012}, and $\\alpha$-arachin \\autocite{cui_peanut_2023}, respectively. All triglycerides were assumed to be triolein.\n\nSee \\nameref{sec:physical-constants} for a complete list of sourced molar masses for these nutrients, and other relevant physical constants used in the model.\n\n\\subsection{Organ Pipeline Model}\n\\autoref{tab:organ-metabolizers} shows a list of all considered organs/organelles, alongside each metabolic process within that organ, and the processed substrates.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]                                                              \n  \\centering                                                                    \n  \\caption{Organs, Metabolizers, and Metabolites in the Peanut Metabolism Simulator}\n  \\label{tab:organ-metabolizers}                                                \n  \\small % Recommended to help fit 4 columns comfortably\n  \\begin{tabularx}{\\textwidth}{|l|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|}                                          \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  \\textbf{Organ} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolizers} \u0026 \\textbf{Transported Metabolites} \u0026 \\textbf{Internal Metabolites} \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  mouth \u0026 salivary amylase \u0026 starch, maltose \u0026 -- \\\\                            \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  stomach \u0026 gastric amylase, pepsin \u0026 starch, maltose, arachin, polypeptide \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  small intestine \u0026 pancreatic amylase, trypsin, lipase \u0026 starch, maltose, polypeptide, peptide chain, triolein, DAG, MAG, oleic acid \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  small intestine lining \u0026 membrane maltose hydrolysis, peptidase, mixed micelle former \u0026 maltose, glucose, peptide chain, glutamic acid, bile salt, micelle \u0026 MAG, oleic acid \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  enterocytes \u0026 golgi (chylomicron producer) \u0026 micelle, chylomicron \u0026 -- \\\\     \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  pancreas \u0026 insulin production \u0026 insulin \u0026 glucose, glutamic acid \\\\           \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  liver \u0026 bile salt producer \u0026 bile salt \u0026 -- \\\\                                \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  blood \u0026 lipoprotein lipase, glucose uptake regulator \u0026 glucose, insulin, chylomicron, oleic acid, glycerol, glutamic acid \u0026 chylomicron remnant \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  cytosol \u0026 glycolysis, acyl-CoA synthase \u0026 glucose, pyruvate, oleic acid, fatty acyl-CoA \u0026 G6P, F6P, F16BP, 3PG, PEP \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  mitochondrial matrix \u0026 alanine amino transferase, TCA cycle, fatty acid oxidation \u0026 pyruvate, glutamic acid, fatty acyl-CoA, alanine, $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate \u0026 NADH, FADH$_2$, GTP, acetyl-CoA \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  mitochondrial inner membrane \u0026 electron transport chain \u0026 NADH, FADH$_2$, ATP \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  \\end{tabularx}                                                                 \n\\end{table}                                                                \n                           \n\nTODO: in table caption, note that the term \"organ\" was applied loosely\n\nThe organs in \\autoref{tab:organ-metabolizers} were organized in a directional, pseudo-acyclic graph structure. Such that, in each simulation time step, an organ receives input metabolites from in its predecessor (for example, the small intestine takes maltose from the stomach). After processing, the organ outputs relevant substrates downstream (i.e. the small intestine outputs glucose to the intestinal lining). This graph structure is demonstrated in TODO: insert image from graph.\n\nThis graph-based pipeline is not fully acyclic because the blood acts as an input, output, and regulatory aid for other organs. In this model, for example, the pancreas, outputs insulin to the bloodstream. However, it also much access the quantities (e.g. input) of glucose and amino acids within the blood to produce the correct amount of insulin \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}.\n\nEach listed organ is treated as a fully isolated node, which only receives inputs as discrete events. No continuous passage of substrates occurs in the model.\n\n\\subsection{Declarative Model Organization}\nWithin code, the model is designed to have a declarative structure, such that functioning code also logically outlines the organization of the human body. Each organ is represented as an object that contains its metabolic agents and rate-limiting functions (\\autoref{fig:code-a}). Each metabolic agent contains a list of its substrates and regulators (\\autoref{fig:code-b}). To create the graph structure, organs are placed within nodes, alongside edges including output metabolites and their destinations (\\autoref{fig:code-c}). \n\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n     \\centering\n     % --- First Row ---\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{organ.png}\n         \\caption{organelle with metabolic processes}\n         \\label{fig:code-a}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n     \\hfill % This pushes the two images to the outer edges\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{metabolic-process.png}\n         \\caption{metabolic process with substrates}\n         \\label{fig:code-b}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n\n     \\vspace{0.5cm} % Adds vertical space between the rows\n\n     % --- Second Row ---\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.96\\textwidth} % You can adjust the width (e.g., 0.7 or 1.0)\n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{organ-node.png}\n         \\caption{edge connecting outputs of organelle to another organelle}\n         \\label{fig:code-c}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n     \n     \\caption{Example, declarative instantiations of physical metabolic organization within code, using the mitochondria (a) , action of alanine amino-transferase (b), and passage of NADH and FADH2 to the inner mitochondrial membrane (c) }\n     \\label{fig:three_images_layout}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Dynamics}\nThe simulation executes in discrete time steps of one minute. This step size was chosen because all reviewed publications of metabolism kinetics expressed rates in minutes.\n\nIn each time step, the above organ graph is traversed in a breadth-first search. In the first time step, initial peanut macronutrients (\\autoref{sec:micronutrient-inputs}) are input directly to the mouth. Then, the mouth executes kinetics for all contained metabolic agents (none), and places destination organs for all output edges (only the stomach) into a queue. The next organ-node is removed from the queue, its metabolic agents executed, and destination organs added to the queue. This process iterates until all organs have been visited.\n\nFor all future time steps, the above process repeats, starting with the mouth. However, nutrients are only input into the mouth in the first time step, modeling a single mouthful of peanuts ingested all at once.\n\n\\section{Explanation of Mathematical Models}\nFrom reviewed kinetics publications, first-order and pseudo-first-order \\autocite{noauthor_281_2013} exponential models were common. Others presented a form of the Michaelis-Menten equation, a popular representation for enzymatic kinetics \\autocite{srinivasan_guide_2022}. Ten such models were employed within the simulation. Most models were adopted assuming that the reaction would never be limited by availability of the enzyme, but only the concentration of substrate.\n\nNot all reactions throughout the simulation used a tested model from the literature. Instead, some included naive approaches to facilitate demonstration of the end-to-end pipeline. \\autoref{tab:metabolizer-kinetics} summarizes the substrates, enzymes, and kinetics equation type for each metabolic process. Below, the formulation of each process in the table is detailed.\n\nAll reaction models were solved numerically using a first-order, ordinary differential equations solver (see \\nameref{sec:software-design}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]                                                                                          \n  \\centering                                                                                                                  \n  \\caption{Metabolizers, Metabolites, and Kinetic Equations}                                                                  \n  \\label{tab:metabolizer-kinetics}                                                                                        \n  \\small % Highly recommended for this table due to the length of the glycolysis row\n  \\begin{tabularx}{\\textwidth}{|l|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|}                                                                                               \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  \\textbf{Metabolizer} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolites} \u0026 \\textbf{Equation Type} \u0026 \\textbf{Source}\\\\                                                                                                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                \n  salivary amylase \u0026 starch $\\rightarrow$ maltose \u0026 first-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}\\\\                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  gastric amylase \u0026 starch $\\rightarrow$ maltose \u0026 first-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012} \\\\                                                                                                                     \n  \\hline                                                                                                                        \n  pepsin \u0026 arachin $\\rightarrow$ polypeptide \u0026 first-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022} \\\\                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  stomach emptying regulator \u0026 starch, maltose, polypeptide, triolein \u0026 exponential power-law \u0026 \\autocite{siegel_biphasic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}\\\\                                                                                                 \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  pancreatic amylase \u0026 starch $\\rightarrow$ maltose \u0026 first-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012} \\\\                                                                                                                      \n  \\hline                                                                                                                       \n  trypsin \u0026 polypeptide $\\rightarrow$ peptide chain \u0026 zero-order \u0026 \\autocite{schwert_kinetics_1949}\\\\                                                           \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  lipase \u0026 triolein $\\rightarrow$ DAG $\\rightarrow$ MAG $\\rightarrow$ oleic acid \u0026 sequential first-order \u0026 \\autocite{lykidis_kinetics_1995}\\\\                                                                                  \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  membrane maltose hydrolysis \u0026 maltose $\\rightarrow$ glucose \u0026 Michaelis-Menten \u0026 \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_2021}\\\\                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                \n  peptidase \u0026 peptide chain $\\rightarrow$ glutamic acid \u0026 first-order \\\\                                                   \n  \\hline                                                                                                                  \n  mixed micelle former \u0026 MAG + oleic acid + bile salt $\\rightarrow$ micelle \u0026 limiting reactant \\\\                                                                                             \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  chylomicron producer \u0026 micelle $\\rightarrow$ chylomicron \u0026 first-order \\\\                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  bile salt producer \u0026 $\\rightarrow$ bile salt \u0026 zero-order \\\\                                                              \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  insulin production \u0026 glucose + glutamic acid $\\rightarrow$ insulin \u0026 linear with feedback \u0026 \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}\\\\                                                                                                     \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  lipoprotein lipase \u0026 chylomicron $\\rightarrow$ chylomicron remnant + oleic acid + glycerol \u0026 zero-order \\\\                                                                                       \n  \\hline                                                                                                                 \n  glucose uptake regulator \u0026 glucose, insulin \u0026 Michaelis-Menten + Hill \u0026 \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_2016}\\\\                                                                                                                          \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  insulin breakdown \u0026 insulin $\\rightarrow$ \u0026 first-order decay \u0026 \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}\\\\                                                            \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  glycolysis \u0026 glucose $\\rightarrow$ G6P $\\rightarrow$ F6P $\\rightarrow$ F16BP $\\rightarrow$ 3PG $\\rightarrow$ PEP $\\rightarrow$ pyruvate \u0026 Michaelis-Menten + Hill \u0026 \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}\\\\                                                                                                        \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  acyl-CoA synthase \u0026 oleic acid $\\rightarrow$ fatty acyl-CoA \u0026 first-order \\\\                                                                                                                      \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                            \n  alanine amino transferase \u0026 glutamic acid + pyruvate $\\leftrightarrow$ $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate + alanine \u0026 first-order \\\\                                                                          \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  TCA cycle \u0026 pyruvate, acetyl-CoA, $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate $\\rightarrow$ NADH, FADH$_2$, GTP \u0026 first-order \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                                                              \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  fatty acid oxidation \u0026 fatty acyl-CoA $\\rightarrow$ acetyl-CoA + NADH + FADH$_2$ \u0026 first-order \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  electron transport chain \u0026 NADH + FADH$_2$ $\\rightarrow$ ATP \u0026 Michaelis-Menten \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                  \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  \\end{tabularx}                                                                                                               \n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Mouth}\nThe mouth is assumed to fully empty its contents into the stomach within a single, one-minute time step. The presence of the esophagus between the mouth and stomach is omitted.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amylase}\n\\label{sec:amylase}\nFor simplicity, starch is treated as 100 percent amylose, which is converted to maltose by amylolysis. However, real starch metabolism would also include a pathway to catabolize contained amylopectin \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Real amylolysis would also produce maltotriose and α-limit dextrins \\autocite{roberts_mechanism_1960, butterworth_enzyme_2022}. \n\nThe mouth contains salivary amylase, and other types of amylase are included in organs below. All amylase reactions were modeled after the same kinetics for pancreatic amylase, a first-order exponential with a rate constant of 0.03 (or starch half-life of 23 minutes) \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Because a model of amylase action specifically on peanuts was not available, the results provided by Butterworth et al. (2012) for beans was selected. Peanuts and beans are in the same legume family, and share similarities in macronutrients \\autocite{toomer_nutritional_2018}.\n\n\\subsection{Stomach}\n\\subsubsection{Gastric Amylase}\nThe same model presented in \\autoref{sec:amylase} acts within the stomach on remaining starch passed to the stomach from the mouth.\n\n\\subsubsection{Pepsin}\nAs no protein catabolism was considered within the mouth, all proteins (arachins) are passed directly to the stomach. Pepsin converts arachin into arachin polypeptides. As a proxy for this reaction, a model fitting the hydrolysis of milk casein by pepsin was used, with a first-order exponential rate constant of 0.08 (protein half life of approximately 9 minutes) \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}.\n\nEffects on the reaction from pepsin denaturation were not considered. Though, other models have considered this effect, particularly for increased denaturation at pH levels over 5.0 \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}. Stomach acidity may approach this level after ingestion of a meal \\autocite{noauthor_regulation_2006}.\n\nOur model further assumed that pepsin was already active in the stomach upon food ingestion, ignoring preliminary activation of pepsinogen to pepsin by hydrochloric acid in the within the stomach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Stomach Empyting Regulator}\nAfter nutrients in the stomach have undergone noted enzymatic reactions and formed into acid chyme, they are emptied into the duodenum of the small intestine. This step is tightly controlled via the pyloric sphincter, at a potential rate of 2-4 kilocalories per minute \\autocite{kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nOur simulation used a power exponential function reviewed by Kong et al. (2008). This model resembled other first-order exponential models discussed, but included a power constant to account for an initial lag period in the emptying of solid foods. With this model, 50 percent of solids are expected to be emptied in approximately 94 minutes, and completely emptied in 2 or more hours \\autocite{siegel_biphasic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nWhile this model did not differentiate between distinct macronutrients,  in a real stomach, a striation of chyme would be expected, with emulsified fats on top. Our simulation did not account for such separation, and instead assumed that all nutrients were distributed and emptied in equal proportion.\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine}\n\\subsubsection{Pancreatic Amylase}\nThe model for pancreatic amylase matched details of \\autoref{sec:amylase}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Trypsin}\nTo simplify, trypsin was the only enzyme considered in converting polypeptides within the small intestine into small peptide chains. However, a more robust model should consider also the presence chymotripsin and carboxypeptidase in this step.\n\nOur model based the catabolic rate of polypeptides on findings by Schwert and Eisenberg (1949) of the action of trypsin on benzoyl-L-argininamide. While the authors noted that the reaction theoretically requires higher-order modeling due to varying levels of competitive inhibition depending on substrate concenration, experimental results followed a zero-order (linear) hydrolysis rate of 1.1e-3 millimoles per minute of polypeptides \\autocite{schwert_kinetics_1949}. This same zero-order model was selected for simulation.\n\n\\subsubsection{Lipase}\nLipases which catalyze conversion of triglycerides to di- and mono-glycerides are present in the mouth, stomach, small intestine, and intestinal lining. Because most lipolysis occurs within the small intestine after emulsification via bile salts (not available to the stomach or mouth), only lipase within the small intestine (pancreatic lipase) was considered.\n\nThe selected model of pancreatic lipase presented distinct first-order reactions for hydrolysis of triolein to 1,2-diolein, and then 1,2-diolein to 2-monolein. Each reaction was dependent on the initial concentration of triolein, and rate constants for both substeps. Dioglycerides were catabolized nearly three times faster than trioglycerides \\autocite{lykidis_kinetics_1995}.\n\n\\subsection{Small-Intestinal-Lining}\n\\subsubsection{Maltose Hydrolysis}\nMaltose resulting from amylolysis earlier in the organ pipeline is hydrolized to glucose. This reaction is catalized by maltase. A Michaelis-Menten equation model was selected, whose numerical parameters were derived from experiments with rat small jejenums \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_2021}. \n\nTo apply this model to humans, a typical human small intestine length \\autocite{fish_physiology_2025} and fed intestinal fluid volume \\autocite{schiller_intestinal_2005} replaced values used for rats.\n\nTODO: Looks like maxHydrolysisRate is in ml. Need to fix units to compare this to mmol\n\n\\subsubsection{Peptidase}\nPeptidase catalyzes the hydrolysis reaction converting peptide chains into individual amino acids (all assumed to be glutamic acid). \n\nThe rate for this step is not sourced. For demonstration, it is assumed to be first-order, catabolizing 20 percent of existing small peptide chains during each time step.\n\n\\subsubsection{Mixed Micelle Formation}\nTo enter the enterocytes, monoglycerides and free fatty acids within the small intestinal lining first form emulsified mixed micelles with bile salts. \n\nKinetics for this process are not sourced. A naive model was implemented, in which a single mole of monolein, two moles or oleic acid, and one mole of bile coalesce to form a mole of micelle. The lowest-quantity substrate was selected as the limiting reactant in each time step, and the reaction consumed 50 percent of the limiting reactant, alongside corresponding proportions of both other substrates.\n\n\\subsubsection{Chylomicron Producer}\nAfter passing into the enterocytes, glycerides and fatty acids form alongside \n\nTODO: insert a bit more info about process to form chylomicrons\n\nTODO: outline native structure\n\nTODO: note why enterocytes are treated as a distinct organ from the small intestinal lining\n\n\\subsection{Liver}\n\\subsubsection{Bile Salt Production}\nTODO: Gall bladder and bile duct are omitted\n\n\\subsection{Pancreas}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Production}\n\\label{sec:insulin-production}\nInsulin production by the pancreas, and secretion to the bloodstream, relied on a model with both plasma glucose, and plasma amino acids, as regulators. To determine the needed level of insulin in a time step, the model assigned linear coefficients to current levels of glucose, amino acids, and insulin. Those coefficients were applied to the differences between current levels and assumed base levels, such that a higher change from base levels corresponded to a higher change in insulin.\n\\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}.\n\nWe adopted this model by communicating calculated values of glucose and glutamic acid within the simulated bloodstream to the pancreas node, which were used as parameters in the provided linear equation.\n\n\\subsection{Bloodstream}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Degradation}\nThe same model used in \\autoref{sec:insulin-production} accounted for insulin degradation within the blood via first-order feedback. Assuming a steady-state average insulin level $I_b$ of approximately 7.5 $mU/ml$, and a rate coefficient $k_I$ of 0.14, insulin in the next time step was denatured by \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}:\n\n\\textbf{\n    Insulin(t) -\u003d k_I * (Insulin(t - 1) - I_b)\n}\n\nIn this manner, insulin was expected to naturally degrade to its base level in steady state. The specific kinetics of the insulin-degrading enzyme were not included in this approach, which contribue to an expected insulin half-life of 4-6 minutes \\autocite{noauthor_chapter_2009}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glucose Uptake Regulator}\nInsulin levels in the blood affect the rate at which glucose is absorbed into cells. Passage of glucose from the blood across the cell membrane was implemented as a one-way edge between the blood and cytosol nodes. Glucose uptake regulation was implemented as a rate limiter for this edge.\n\nEstimated parameters were selected from a Michaelis-Menten equation of glucose uptake, fit to glucose and insulin readings of human adults. This equation modeled the maximum glucose uptake per unit time as a Hill function \\autocite{goutelle_hill_2008} of insulin concentration at the active site \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_2016}. \n\n\\subsection{Within cells}\nDistinct areas of cells, including the cytosol, mitochondrial matrix, and inner mitochondrial membrane, were modeled as though the volume of participating cells of the body were combined into a single cell, with equal distribution of nutrients from the blood to all areas. This volume was visualized as single, pseudo-organ nodes for each area.\n\nTypes of cells were not differentiated, and a total count of 30.00e12 cells participating in glycolysis and energy generation was assumed.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glycolysis - Cytosol}\nGlucose which enters the cell cytosol undergoes glycolysis, with the final output of pyruvate. The simulation assumed only aerobic conditions, and, hence, that zero pyruvate was ever later reduced to lactic acid. \n\nFurther, glycolysis, and other below catabolic processes within the cells, treated adenosine-triphosphate (ATP) levels as effectively unlimited. Their availability was not considered for substrate inputs, nor outputs (excluding the electron transport chain [ETC].)\n\nThe selected model considered seven different intermediate substrates, in order of appearance in glycolysis: glucose, glucose-6-phosphate (G6P), fructose-6-phosphate (F6P), 3-phosphoglycerate (3PG), phosphenolpyruvate (PEP), and pyruvate. The rate of change of each was modeled with Michaelis-Menten and Hill kinetics as a function of other intermediates \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}.\n\n\\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} shows highly-simplified relationships between each of these substrates. While the table omits feedback effects on particular glycolytic enzymes, expected examples of feed-forward an -backward regulation are present. For example, F16P increases the later production of PEP from 3PG (hence increasing PEP and decreasing 3PG) by allosterically activating pyruvate kinase, which catalyzes this reaction \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}. As a backwards-feedback example, G6P\u0027s inhibition of hexokinase, which catalyzes conversion of glucose to G6P, is encoded in \\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} as \"self-regulation\".\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Glycolysis ODE Metabolite Dependencies}\n\\label{tab:glycolysis-dependencies}\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.3} % Better spacing for readability\n\\begin{NiceTabular}{llc}[\n    code-before \u003d {\n        % This tells LaTeX: Start at row 2, alternate gray and white, \n        % but only apply it to columns 2 and 3.\n        \\rowcolors{2}{gray!10}{white}[cols\u003d{2-3}]\n    }\n]\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metabolite} \u0026 \\textbf{Dependency} \u0026 \\textbf{Effect} \\\\\n\\midrule\n\nGLC (Intracellular Glucose) \u0026 GLC \u0026 $\\pm$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{3-1}{G6P (Glucose-6-Phosphate)} \n    \u0026 GLC \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{2-1}{F6P (Fructose-6-Phosphate)} \n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{2-1}{F16BP (Fructose-1,6-Bisphosphate)} \n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{3-1}{3PG (3-Phosphoglycerate)} \n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{3-1}{PEP (Phosphoenolpyruvate)} \n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 $-$  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{2-1}{Pyruvate} \n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{NiceTabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nOur simulation lacked key glycolysis regulation steps due to omission of non-substrate regulators. In particular, no enzymes were regulated by presence of energy-yielding molecules, such as ATP, adenosine di-phosphate (ADP), and adenosine-mono-phosphate (AMP).\n\n\\subsubsection{Fatty Acid Oxidation -- Mitochondrial Matrix}\nBelow reactions within the mitochondrial matrix are implemented naively in that input from previous steps were assumed to be converted directly into energy-yielding molecules. However, net quantities of energy-yielding molecules output were expected to be realistic.\n\nTo retain a pool of cycle inputs for visualization, only 75 percent were processed in each time step for fatty acid oxidation, conversion of amino acids to alanine, the TCA Cycle.\n\n1mol of Fatty acyl-CoA produced a mol of acetyl-CoA based on the length of the fatty acid carbon chain:\n\n\\textbf{Acetyl-CoA \u003d N_C / 2 - 1}\n\nin addition 1mol NADH and 1mol FADH2 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}.\n\nAs oleic acid has a carbon length of 18 \\autocite{pubchem_oleic_nodate}, each step produced 8mol Acetyl-CoA per mol of oleic acid.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amino Acid Catabolism - Mitochondrial Matrix}\nGlutamic acid and pyruvate react to form a-ketoglutarate and alanine, when catalyzed by alanine-aminotransferase \\autocite{lenartowicz_significance_1988}. a-ketoglutarate can then enter the TCA cycle \\autoref{sec:tca}\n\nOur model assumed that glutamic acid would always be the limiting substrate of this reaction, such that, in each time step, 75 percent of contained glutamic acid is consumed, along with an equal number of mols of pyruvate.\n\n\n\\subsubsection{TCA Cycle - Mitochondrial Matrix}\n\\label{sec:tca}\nEach mol of acetyl-CoA and pyruvate (intermediate conversion to oxaloacetate omitted) produced 3mol NADH, 1mol FADH2, and 1mol guanosine-triphosphate (GTP). Ketogluratate produced 2mol NADH, 1mol FADH2, and 1mol GTP. \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\n\nThe mitochondrial matrix included two processes with the same input substrate -- both amino acid catabolism and TCA required pyruvate. Distribution of pyruvate to these two processes was implemented via a simple, ordered list. First, all available glutamate was converted. Then, all remaining pyruvate was passed to the numerical solver for TCA.\n\n\\subsubsection{ETC - Mitochondrial Inner Membrane}\nThe ETC model assumed that 2mol ATP were produced per mol NADH, and 1.5mol per mol FADH2. The maximum production rate was ATP 100 molecules per minute, per ATP-synthase \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. Total production considered total number of active synthases as a product of synthases per mitochondria, mitochondria per cell, and total cells (\\nameref{sec:physical-constants}).\n\n\n\\section{Appendix I: Physical Constants} \n\\label{sec:physical-constants}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Physical Constants Used in Metabolism Simulation}\n\\label{tab:physical-constants}\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.3} % Matches the spacing of the previous table\n\\begin{NiceTabular}{lllll}[\n    code-before \u003d {\n        % Zebra striping for all rows starting from the header\n        \\rowcolors{1}{gray!10}{white}\n        % Manually highlighting the header and sub-category rows